# THE LOCAL, PROD-SHAPED PROFILE — `*.cafaye.test` hostnames, oauth-stub as the
# social provider.
#
#   config/local.env        THIS FILE — the surface every local run shares.
#   config/.env.local       GENERATED, GITIGNORED — the key material (see below).
#   bin/local-idp           brings the loop up, tears it down, prints the proofs.
#
# `source config/local.env && source config/.env.local` is the whole of the
# configuration interface; `bin/local-idp` does that plus generates what is
# missing. There is no code path in `internal/config` that reads this file — it
# is an operator input, and `internal/config/localprofile_test.go` is the check
# that holds it to what a real `config.Load` produces.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS RATHER THAN A CODE DEFAULT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `OIDC_ISSUER` has no default and must not get one. AGENTS.md is explicit that
# a silent default is "how a service ends up listening on the wrong port in
# production", and an issuer is worse than a port: it is baked into every token
# this service ever signs, and PLAN §1 fixes it as a stability rule that is
# never moved after first issuance. A process that defaulted its issuer would be
# a process that mints tokens for a deployment nobody chose.
#
# So the prod-shaped value lives HERE, in a file an operator sources on purpose,
# and the local run is a deliberate choice rather than a fallback. The `.test`
# hostnames are the local projection of the production names in PLAN §1 — same
# split, same paths, different TLD, so nothing that works locally is working by
# accident of a name that only exists in one place.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THERE ARE NO SECRETS IN THIS FILE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The private key and the OAuth encryption key are deliberately absent, and
# their absence makes the file INVALID on its own: `loadOIDC` counts
# OIDC_ISSUER, OIDC_SIGNING_KEY and OIDC_SIGNING_KEY_ID and refuses anything
# other than all three or none. Sourcing this file alone is therefore a refusal
# to boot naming the variable — which is the rule this repository already
# applies to every other secret, applied to a profile instead of to a typo.
#
# `bin/local-idp` generates `config/.env.local` on first run (an RSA key for the
# provider, a 32-byte key for the OAuth token cipher) and it is gitignored by
# the `.env.*` rule in `.gitignore`. A key committed to this repository would be
# a key in every clone and every image layer forever, and a local signing key is
# not worth that: `bin/local-idp keygen` makes a new one in under a second.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE PORTS, and why they are not the production ones
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 19443 (TLS edge), 19080 (identity), 19081/19082 (the two oauth-stubs),
# 19090 (gateway). The `parlor-e2e-*` stack owns 16xxx and kit's stack owns
# lower still; nothing here collides with either, and a developer can run this
# loop beside a running e2e stack without either noticing the other.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE PROVIDER
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The issuer is `https`, and it is https even where the transport certificate is
# a locally generated one. That is not sloppiness about TLS: PLAN §1 fixes the
# issuer as `https://auth.cafaye.com`, the value is baked into every token
# forever, and a local process that minted `http://…` tokens would be practising
# a shape no verifier on the platform will ever accept. `OIDC_ALLOW_INSECURE`
# stays UNSET because the scheme is https — it is the escape hatch for an http
# issuer, and this profile does not want one.
OIDC_ISSUER=https://auth.cafaye.test

# The `kid` every locally minted token carries. It is a local name, not a
# production one, and it changes when `bin/local-idp keygen` runs: a verifier
# that cached the old JWKS is a verifier that has cached a local test key, and
# a new key means it must re-fetch.
OIDC_SIGNING_KEY_ID=local-cafaye-test-1

# Where the sign-in page lives. identity renders no HTML (DECISIONS.md D10) and
# refuses to start a provider without this, so the local profile points it at
# the app origin — the same origin the session cookie has to land on, which is
# the property the social round trip exists to measure.
OIDC_LOGIN_UI_URL=https://cafaye.test/login

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE PUBLIC FACE, AND THE TWO VARIABLES THAT HAVE TO AGREE WITH IT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# This service's own public base URL. `oauth.Registry` builds every provider
# callback from it and from NOTHING else — never from the request's Host — so
# this is the value the whole social round trip is really about. Set it to the
# identity face and set `OIDC_ISSUER` to anything else and the round trip
# completes against the stub and delivers the code to an origin the browser is
# not signed in on, which is the deployment failure oauth-stub exists to catch.
OAUTH_REDIRECT_BASE_URL=https://auth.cafaye.test

# Turning social login on locally is choosing the two providers the stub
# impersonates. These are the stub's credentials, not Google's and not GitHub's:
# oauth-stub holds exactly one client pair per instance and refuses anything
# else, so a mismatch here is a `invalid_client` from the stub rather than a
# confusing failure inside identity.
OAUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=local-cafaye-google-client
OAUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=local-cafaye-google-secret
OAUTH_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=local-cafaye-github-client
OAUTH_GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=local-cafaye-github-secret

# The stub's endpoints, reached over the loopback — NOT over `auth.cafaye.test`.
# This is the sharp edge in the profile and it is deliberate: the AUTHORIZE URL
# is the one the browser is sent to, so it is the one address that has to be
# reachable from the browser's own resolver, while the token and userinfo URLs
# are only ever fetched by identity's server side, for which `localhost` is the
# honest spelling. Pointing the authorize URL at `localhost` and the others at
# a `.test` name would work, and pointing all four at `localhost` would work
# too — what would NOT work is pointing any of them at a host the browser
# cannot resolve, which is why the split is written down instead of left to be
# discovered.
OAUTH_GOOGLE_AUTHORIZE_URL=http://localhost:19081/google/authorize
OAUTH_GOOGLE_TOKEN_URL=http://localhost:19081/google/token
OAUTH_GOOGLE_USERINFO_URL=http://localhost:19081/google/userinfo
OAUTH_GITHUB_AUTHORIZE_URL=http://localhost:19082/github/authorize
OAUTH_GITHUB_TOKEN_URL=http://localhost:19082/github/access_token
OAUTH_GITHUB_USERINFO_URL=http://localhost:19082/github/user
OAUTH_GITHUB_EMAILS_URL=http://localhost:19082/github/user/emails

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE PROCESS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

PORT=19080
LOG_LEVEL=debug

# The database. `bin/local-idp` starts its own container-free Postgres only if
# one is not already reachable at this address; the local loop is otherwise the
# same dogfood path `bin/dev` takes.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://cafaye:cafaye@127.0.0.1:15500/identity?sslmode=disable

# The courier token and the MFA key are absent on purpose: the local loop sends
# no mail and mounts no second factor, and both are optional features. A
# recovery link rendered without a courier is still asserted by the unit tier,
# which builds its own mailer.
